# {{.ProviderName}} Provider

The Zyxel provider manages router configuration directly through the router's
encrypted `DAL` HTTP API. It needs no CLI, Python, or agent at runtime.

> **Status: experimental.** Read paths and mock-router lifecycle tests pass, and
> a read-only plan has been verified against a physical router. Live writes have
> not been verified on hardware. Review every plan and keep a router backup.

## Requirements

- Terraform 1.14 or newer
- Go 1.27 or newer, only when building the provider from source

## Installation

The provider is published on the
[Terraform Registry](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/VincentSchmid/zyxel/latest).
Declare it in your configuration:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    zyxel = {
      source  = "VincentSchmid/zyxel"
      version = "~> 6.1"
    }
  }
}
```

```sh
terraform init
```

Terraform downloads the published binary; no Go toolchain or local build is needed.
Remove any existing `dev_overrides` entry for this provider to use the published
version. For local development, see the
[development instructions](https://github.com/VincentSchmid/terraform-provider-zyxel#development).

## Example Usage

All examples use illustrative names, locally administered MAC addresses, reserved
documentation IP ranges (`192.0.2.0/24`, `198.51.100.0/24`, `203.0.113.0/24`,
and `2001:db8::/32`), and the reserved `example.test` domain. Replace them with
values for your network before use. Interface paths, management access rules,
and ACL positions must be selected for your router; examples are not a complete
security policy. A provider block with no arguments uses `ZYXEL_HOST`,
`ZYXEL_USER`, and `ZYXEL_PASS` from the environment.

```hcl
provider "zyxel" {}

data "zyxel_status" "router" {}

resource "zyxel_nat_rule" "https" {
  name              = "HTTPS"
  protocol          = "tcp"
  external_ports    = "443"
  internal_client   = "192.0.2.20"
  translation_ports = "443"
}

resource "zyxel_dns_record" "host" {
  hostname     = "host.example.test"
  ipv4_address = "192.0.2.20"
}

resource "zyxel_dhcp_reservation" "host" {
  mac_address  = "02:00:00:00:00:20"
  ipv4_address = zyxel_dns_record.host.ipv4_address
}

# Allow the published Cloudflare ranges to reach the example HTTPS host.
# These accepts alone do not deny other sources. For an ordered allow/drop
# policy, use zyxel_acl_policy as described in the ACL ordering section.
# Each accept appends from the current table; no numeric order is configured.
data "zyxel_cloudflare_ipv4_ranges" "https" {}

resource "zyxel_acl_rule" "cloudflare" {
  for_each = toset(data.zyxel_cloudflare_ipv4_ranges.https.ranges)

  name              = "Cloudflare-${replace(each.value, "/", "-")}"
  action            = "accept"
  protocol          = "tcp"
  source_cidr       = each.value
  destination_cidr  = "192.0.2.20/32"
  destination_ports = "443"
}
```

## Credentials

Set the complete pair through provider configuration or through both
`ZYXEL_USER` and `ZYXEL_PASS`; the provider never mixes a configured value with
an environment value. `ZYXEL_HOST` supplies the host. The provider does not read
`.env` files or credentials from disk.

The router firmware limits concurrent administrator logins. Terraform runs
resource operations one after another for that endpoint. Operations sharing a
normalized endpoint in this process are serialized, including separate clients.
Default parallelism is supported, but use exclusive router access: endpoint
aliases, separate Terraform processes and UI users are not coordinated by the
process-local lock.

## Firewall Services

Use `zyxel_service` to manage reusable TCP, UDP, ICMP, ICMPv6, or other IP
protocol objects and reference them with `zyxel_acl_rule.service_id`. Both
services and inline ACLs accept compact `source_ports` and `destination_ports`
selectors such as `"*"`, `"443"`, and `"443-445"`.

Service deletion is refused while any ACL references it, to prevent the router
from cascading deletion to associated rules. See the service resource docs for
replacement safety and examples.

## Ordered ACL Policies

An ordered accept list followed by required drops/rejects needs
`zyxel_acl_policy`: individual `zyxel_acl_rule` resources cannot maintain that
trailing protection across collection changes, even with `depends_on` or
`-parallelism=1`. Policy rules are validated individually and may mix protocols,
services, address families, directions and ports; each rule may reference a
custom `service` name or `service_id` instead of an inline `protocol` and ports.
Read [ACL ordering, limitations and recovery](guides/acl-ordering-recovery.md)
before using policies, migrating rows or retrying partial applies.

